# A statement whose Account column holds BANK-SPEAK, not an account name.
#
# This is the shape the whole `alias` feature exists for. A real Morgan Stanley
# export names the account `PW Roth IRA - 3077`, and interpolating that column
# into `account1` puts the bank's own words straight into the journal. The user's
# `alias` directive is what turns it into `assets:morganstanley:pw-roth-ira` —
# but ONLY when it reaches hledger as `--alias`, because an alias directive
# sitting in the target journal does not reach a CSV during an import (verified
# against hledger 1.52; see crates/ledgeline-core/src/aliases.rs).
#
# `%account:cash` rather than `%account` on purpose: it proves that a PREFIX
# alias rewrites the base and leaves the `:cash` leaf intact, which is what
# avoids needing one alias per account-and-subaccount combination.

skip 1
fields date, account, description, amount
date-format %m/%d/%Y
currency $

account1 %account:cash
account2 income:unknown

if DIVIDEND REINVEST
    account2 income:dividends

if ADVISORY FEE
    account2 expenses:fees
